"# Review log, round 2: communities\n\nIndependent fact-check of canon/canon_communities.csv (300 rows, kind `community`).\nBackup of the pre-review file: archive/prereview_round2/canon_communities.csv.\nScope (196 rows): all 115 R1/R2 rows (endonym check); all 33 rows with endonym/self-name \"to verify\"; all 9 rows carrying a \"(rejected exonym)\" flag plus a search for missing ones; the 11 flagged rows (COM184 Druze, COM191 Baekjeong, COM194 Buraku, COM133 Taíno, COM154 Uyghur, COM220 Mahra, COM221 Soqotri, COM206 Pashtun, COM249 Guanches, COM059 Konso, COM068 Haya); a random sample of 50 further rows (seed 20260929; ids in work_r2com/sel.json). Tense check (making_significance) and sensitivity-flag check run over all 300 rows.\nEdit script: work_r2com/apply.py.\n\nFormat: id | field | old | new | source URL\n\nCOM003 | notes | Shali date traditional; endonym to verify. | Shali date traditional; endonym Isiwan verified. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siwi_people\nCOM012 | name | Ishelhin | Ishelḥiyen | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shilha_people\n",
"COM012 | other_names | Chleuh (exonym); Shilha-speakers; Soussi | Chleuh (French rendering of the self-name); Shilha-speakers; Soussi | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shilha_people\nCOM012 | notes | Endonym form to verify; links: Moroccan Jewish communities row. | Endonym išelḥiyen verified (also written Ishelhien); links: Moroccan Jewish communities row. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shilha_people\nCOM019 | other_names | Senara; Senambele (endonym to verify) | Siena; Syénambélé (Senambele); Senoufo (French); Senara (one subgroup) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senufo_people\nCOM019 | notes | links: PRA027. Endonym to verify. | links: PRA027. The name Senufo is of external origin and subgroups use their own names; no single endonym, so the widely used name is kept (endonym to verify). | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senufo_people\nCOM058 | other_names | Gey Usu | Gēy Usuach (Gey Usu, \"people of the city\") | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harari_people\nCOM058 | notes | Endonym to verify. | Endonym verified: Gēy Usuach. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harari_people\n",
"COM059 | other_names | Xonsitta (endonym to verify) | Xonsita (self-name); language Afaa Xonso | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konso_people\nCOM059 | making_significance | Konso communities build walled terraced towns and carve waka memorial figures, some stolen and later returned. | Konso communities build walled terraced towns and carve waka memorial figures, many of which have been stolen for the art market; Ethiopian customs impounded more than 200 from 1996. | https://mg.co.za/article/2010-01-03-a-museum-to-save-an-ethiopian-tribes-soul/\nCOM059 | notes | Waka thefts documented; return cases to verify. | Endonym Xonsita verified (single t). Seized waka are kept at the Konso Museum; individual returns from abroad still to verify. | https://mg.co.za/article/2010-01-03-a-museum-to-save-an-ethiopian-tribes-soul/\nCOM104 | other_names | Cáuigù | Gáuigú (Kiowa Tribe spelling; also Ǥáuigú, Cáuigú) | https://www.kiowatribe.org/department/kiowa-language-department\n",
"COM104 | notes | links: SCH009. Fort Marion imprisonment: human-flow. Endonym 'Cáuigù' to verify. | links: SCH009. Fort Marion imprisonment: human-flow. Endonym Gáuigú verified with the Kiowa Tribe Language Department. | https://www.kiowatribe.org/department/kiowa-language-department\nCOM104 | making_significance | Kiowa artists kept pictographic calendars and ledger drawings, and the Kiowa Six painters worked in Oklahoma in the 1920s. | Kiowa artists kept pictographic calendars and ledger drawings, the Kiowa Six painters worked in Oklahoma in the 1920s, and Kiowa artists continue to paint today. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiowa\nCOM109 | name | Aniyvwiya | Tsalagi | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherokee\nCOM109 | other_names | Cherokee; Tsalagi | Cherokee; Aniyvwiya (\"Principal People\"); Anigiduwagi (\"people of Kituwah\") | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherokee\nCOM109 | notes | links: POL065. Trail of Tears: human-flow. Endonym forms to verify. | links: POL065. Trail of Tears: human-flow. Tsalagi (ᏣᎳᎩ) is the standard endonym; Aniyvwiya (ᎠᏂᏴᏫᏯ) and Anigiduwagi also used. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherokee\n",
"COM120 | notes | Endonyms to verify. links: CIV028; PRA048. | Endonyms Ben'zaa (Valley, also Bën Za, \"cloud people\") and Binnizá (Isthmus) as listed by native-land.ca and Wikipedia. links: CIV028; PRA048. | https://native-land.ca/listings/territories/didxazon\nCOM137 | notes | links: PRA051; PRA053; CIV022. 'Runakuna' as a self-name to verify. | links: PRA051; PRA053; CIV022. Runa (\"person\"), plural runakuna, verified as the Quechua self-designation. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quechua_people\nCOM138 | other_names | Aymara; Jaqi (endonym to verify) | Aymara; Jaqi (\"human beings\", historical self-designation); Colla | https://www.encyclopedia.com/history/latin-america-and-caribbean/mesoamerican-indigenous-peoples/aymara\nCOM163 | notes | 'Sharwa' endonym to verify. | Endonym Sharwa verified. | https://english.indigenousvoice.com/sherpa\nCOM165 | other_names | Lepcha (exonym) | Lepcha (exonym); Mútuncí Róngkup Rumkup; Rongpa (Sikkimese form) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lepcha_people\nCOM165 | notes | Endonym to verify. | Endonym Rongkup verified. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lepcha_people\nCOM165 | confidence | low | medium | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lepcha_people\n",
"COM197 | notes | links: SCH027. Koitur endonym to verify. | links: SCH027. Endonym Kōītōr (Koitur, Koi) verified; Gond is the name used by outsiders. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gondi_people\nCOM198 | other_names | Hor ko (endonym to verify) | Hor Hopon (\"child of human\"); Hor | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santal_people\nCOM198 | notes |  | Endonym Hor Hopon verified. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santal_people\nCOM202 | other_names | Ki Hynniewtrep | Ki Hynñiewtrep (\"the seven huts\"; collective self-designation of Khasi-Jaintia peoples) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khasi_people\nCOM202 | notes | Endonym to verify. | Hynñiewtrep verified; living root bridges are credited to War Khasi and War-Jaintia communities. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khasi_people\nCOM255 | name | Xal'mg | Khalmgud | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalmyks\nCOM255 | other_names | Kalmyks | Kalmyks; Хальмгуд; Xaľmg (sing./adj.) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalmyks\nCOM255 | notes | Deportation 1943: human-flow. Endonym spelling to verify. | Deportation 28 December 1943: human-flow. Endonym Хальмгуд (Khalmgud) verified. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalmyks\n",
"COM265 | notes | links: CIV093; MOV106. 'Zenadth Kes' usage to verify. | links: CIV093; MOV106. Zenadth Kes is the Torres Strait Islander name for the region, used by Islanders who reject the colonial name. | https://theconversation.com/zenadth-kes-peoples-long-journey-to-self-determination-in-the-torres-strait-199675\nCOM270 | other_names | Ambulas (endonym to verify) | Ambulas (name of their language) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambulas_language\nCOM270 | notes |  | Endonym to verify: Ambulas is the language name, not attested as a self-name for the people. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambulas_language\nCOM280 | name | Ri-Majeļ | Ri-M̧ajeļ | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall_Islands\nCOM280 | other_names | Marshallese | Marshallese; ri-Majol (popular spelling) | https://www.infomarshallislands.com/language/\nCOM280 | notes | links: INV115; PRA203; PLC409. Test displacement: human-flow. Endonym spelling to verify. | links: INV115; PRA203; PLC409. Test displacement: human-flow. M̧ajeļ is the Marshallese name of the islands (standard orthography); ri- = person of. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall_Islands\n",
"COM291 | notes | links: POL084; POL085; MKR088; PRA199. Endonym to verify. | links: POL084; POL085; MKR088; PRA199. Kakai Tonga (\"Tongan people\") in use, e.g. Waikato thesis title \"Kakai Tonga 'i Okalani\". | https://researchcommons.waikato.ac.nz/entities/publication/95d8ed10-9bdb-4990-ada4-575357c182dc\nCOM297 | name | Enana | ʻEnana | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marquesas_Islands\nCOM297 | other_names | Marquesans; Enata | Marquesans; ʻEnata (South Marquesan) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marquesas_Islands\nCOM192 | other_names | Aynu; Utari (term used by some organisations) | Aynu; Utari (\"friend\"; used by the Hokkaido Utari Association 1961–2009, now the Ainu Association of Hokkaido) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ainu_Association_of_Hokkaido\nCOM139 | other_names | Mapuche; Araucanian (exonym) | Mapuche; Araucanian (colonial exonym; considered pejorative by some) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mapuche\nCOM093 | other_names | Kwakiutl (exonym, one community's name) | Kwakiutl (from Kwagu'ł, one community at Fort Rupert, formerly applied to all) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kwakwaka%CA%BCwakw\nCOM182 | name | Ma'dan | ʿArab al-Ahwār | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsh_Arabs\n",
"COM182 | other_names | Marsh Arabs | Marsh Arabs; Ahwaris; Ma'dan (rejected exonym: used disparagingly by desert tribes, often considered derogatory today) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsh_Arabs\nCOM182 | making_significance | Ma'dan build mudhif reed guesthouses and reed boats (mashuf) in the southern Iraqi marshes, which were drained in the 1990s. | Marsh Arab communities build mudhif reed guesthouses and reed boats (mashuf) in the southern Iraqi marshes, which were drained in the 1990s. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsh_Arabs\nCOM182 | notes | Drainage and displacement: human-flow. | Drainage and displacement: human-flow. Name changed from Ma'dan, often considered derogatory. | https://cinmena.org/nations/ma%CA%BBdan-or-marsh-arabs/\nCOM184 | name | Muwahhidun | Muwaḥḥidūn | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druze\nCOM184 | other_names | Druze | Druze (from ad-Darazī, whom the faith regards as a heretic; widely used, including by the community) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druze\n",
"COM184 | making_significance | Druze communities in Lebanon, Syria and Israel keep distinctive dress and build stone village houses and khalwa prayer houses. | Druze communities in Lebanon, Syria, Israel and Jordan keep distinctive religious dress and build stone village houses and khalwa prayer houses. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druze\nCOM184 | confidence | low | medium | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druze\nCOM184 | notes | Endonym 'al-Muwahhidun' (the monotheists); making significance is thin; the brief marked this with a query. | Endonym al-Muwaḥḥidūn (\"the monotheists\") verified; da'wa opened 30 May 1017. Making significance is modest. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Druze\nCOM191 | other_names |  | Paekchŏng (McCune–Reischauer); a status label imposed by the state, with no self-chosen name | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baekjeong\nCOM191 | date_note | hereditary outcaste status under Joseon; legally abolished 1894 | Joseon status category formed under Sejong (r. 1418–1450), who grouped outcaste groups under this name; legally abolished 1894 (Kabo Reform); discrimination continued into the 20th c. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baekjeong\n",
"COM191 | making_significance | Baekjeong were a hereditary low-status group of butchers, leatherworkers and willow-basket makers in Joseon Korea, and their descendants organised the Hyeongpyeongsa movement in 1923. | People classed as baekjeong, a hereditary outcaste status in Joseon Korea, worked as butchers, tanners and willow-basket makers, and in 1923 they founded the Hyeongpyeongsa in Jinju to end the status and its contemptuous names. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baekjeong\nCOM191 | defined_by | the community; ethnographic and museum usage | the Joseon state (legal status category); historians | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baekjeong\nCOM191 | confidence | low | medium | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baekjeong\nCOM191 | notes | Historical status group rather than self-named people; verify framing. | Kind note: a hereditary status imposed by the state and enforced by discrimination, not a people or ethnic group; write \"people classed as baekjeong\". | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baekjeong\n",
"COM194 | other_names | Burakumin (term many find stigmatising) | buraku; hisabetsu buraku (\"discriminated-against communities\"); Burakumin (rejected term: carries strong discriminatory connotations) | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burakumin\nCOM194 | making_significance | Buraku communities, historically assigned to leather and hide work, include makers of taiko drums and leather goods, and the Buraku Liberation League campaigns against discrimination. | Buraku communities, descended from people assigned to hide, leather and other stigmatised work under Edo-period status rules, include makers of taiko drums and leather goods, and the Buraku Liberation League campaigns against the discrimination that persists. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burakumin\nCOM194 | date_note | outcaste status formalised in Edo period; abolished legally 1871 | outcaste status formalised in Edo period; abolished legally by the Liberation Edict of 28 August 1871; discrimination persists | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burakumin\n",
