"COM133 | making_significance | Taíno descendants revive making including cemí carving, and descent is debated. | People who identify as Taíno in Puerto Rico, the Dominican Republic, Cuba and the diaspora carve cemís and duhos as part of a heritage resurgence; many see unbroken descent, citing Indigenous ancestry in present Caribbean DNA and survivals in crops, food and vocabulary, while others hold that the Taíno did not survive as a distinct people after the 16th century. | https://www.smithsonianmag.com/blogs/smithsonian-latino-center/2019/10/25/funeral-caribbeans-native-extinction-hypothesis/\nCOM133 | confidence | low | medium | https://www.smithsonianmag.com/blogs/smithsonian-latino-center/2018/10/03/renaissance-native-caribbean-people-taino-ethnogenesis/\nCOM133 | notes | links: CIV030; POL055. Continuity is debated. | links: CIV030; POL055. Continuity is debated: both views stated (Smithsonian NMAI \"Taíno: Native Heritage and Identity in the Caribbean\" takes the continuity view). | https://www.si.edu/exhibitions/taino-native-heritage-and-identity-caribbean-event-exhib-6313\n",
"COM154 | date_note | modern collective ethnonym adopted 1921; Turkic Uyghur states from 8th c. are separate groupings | modern ethnonym adopted at a 1921 Tashkent meeting of Turkic intellectuals from the Tarim Basin and made official in Xinjiang in 1934 (Sheng Shicai); the people and their oasis identities are older; the Uyghur Khaganate (8th–9th c.) is a separate grouping | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uyghurs\nCOM206 | making_significance | Pashtun communities make embroidered dress and the painted and decorated trucks associated with Pakistan. | Pashtun communities make embroidered dress, and Peshawar and Swat, in the Pashtun north-west, are among the centres of Pakistani truck decoration alongside Karachi, Rawalpindi, Quetta and Lahore. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truck_art_in_South_Asia\nCOM206 | confidence | low | medium | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truck_art_in_South_Asia\nCOM206 | notes | Included with a query in the brief; making claims modest. | Endonym Pax̌tānə (Pashtana, pl.) standard. Truck art is regional and multi-ethnic; not attributed to Pashtuns as a whole. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truck_art_in_South_Asia\n",
"COM220 | making_significance | Mahra communities of eastern Yemen and Dhofar build sewn and planked boats, herd camels and frankincense lands, and make silver. | Mahra communities of eastern Yemen and Dhofar live from camel herding and Arabian Sea fishing, harvested and carried frankincense into the 20th c., and include noted navigators of Indian Ocean trade. | https://whenmelodiesgather.supdigital.org/wmg/geography\nCOM220 | materials_techniques | silver;leather;boat building | camel herding;fishing;frankincense harvesting;navigation | https://whenmelodiesgather.supdigital.org/wmg/geography\nCOM220 | object_types | boat;camel gear;jewellery | boat;camel gear;frankincense | https://whenmelodiesgather.supdigital.org/wmg/geography\nCOM220 | confidence | low | medium | https://whenmelodiesgather.supdigital.org/wmg/geography\nCOM220 | notes |  | Earlier claims of sewn boats and silverwork unverified and removed. Also Mahris (al-Mahra tribe), found also on Socotra and in Oman. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mehri_people\nCOM221 | confidence | low | medium | https://www.soqotraculturalheritage.org/crafts/\n",
"COM221 | notes |  | Pottery made only by women; weaving by women on a horizontal ground loom; dragon's-blood resin applied to pots after firing (Soqotra Heritage Project). | https://www.soqotraculturalheritage.org/crafts/\nCOM249 | date_note | settlement c. 1st millennium BCE; conquest 1402–1496 | settlement date debated: 1st millennium BCE (older dates, e.g. Cueva de los Guanches) or 1st–4th c. CE (Santana et al., PNAS 2024); Castilian conquest 1402–1496 | https://popular-archaeology.com/article/human-colonization-of-the-canary-islands/\nCOM249 | making_significance | The Indigenous islanders made hand-built pottery, pintaderas stamps and goatskin clothing, and many were enslaved after the conquest. | The Indigenous islanders made hand-built pottery, pintadera stamps and goatskin clothing, and during the Castilian conquest many were killed, enslaved and deported. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guanches\nCOM249 | confidence | low | medium | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guanches\n",
"COM249 | notes | Amazigh-related; mummies in museums; enslavement: human-flow. | Amazigh-related; \"Guanche\" first meant the people of Tenerife and is now used for the whole archipelago; mummies in museums; enslavement: human-flow; Guanche ancestry survives in present Canarians. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guanches\nCOM068 | date_note | iron smelting at Kagera dated to early 1st millennium BCE by some studies (contested) | Early Iron Age smelting in Buhaya (Kagera) dated to the mid to later 1st millennium BCE (steel-making c. 2,300–2,000 years ago); precise dates debated | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_metallurgy_in_Africa\nCOM068 | making_significance | Haya smelters reconstructed bloomery furnaces for archaeologist Peter Schmidt in the 1970s, a case of living knowledge meeting archaeology. | Haya elders in Kagera, north-west Tanzania, rebuilt and fired bloomery furnaces for archaeologist Peter Schmidt in the 1970s, a case of living knowledge meeting archaeology, and Haya communities hold this smelting history in oral tradition. | https://archaeologydataservice.ac.uk/archives/view/assemblage/html/4/4schmidt.html\n",
"COM077 | making_significance | Herero women wear and make the long Victorian-derived dress and horned headdress, a response to German colonial rule. | Herero women make and wear long dresses adopted from late-19th-century German missionary fashion, now in bright prints, with the otjikaiva headdress shaped like cattle horns. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herero_people\nCOM299 | start | -500 | -700 | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pontic_Greeks\nCOM190 | making_significance | Koryo-saram, deported to Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan in 1937, adapted Korean food, dress and farming tools to Central Asia. | Koryo-saram, deported to Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan in 1937, adapted Korean food, dress and farming tools to Central Asia, and dishes such as morkovcha are now part of post-Soviet cooking. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koryo-saram\nCOM187 | making_significance | Adyghe gold embroidery and silver belt work, carried to Ottoman lands after the 1864 expulsion. | Adyghe women make gold embroidery and smiths make silver belt work, crafts carried to Ottoman lands after the 1864 expulsion. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circassians\n",
"COM015 | making_significance | Jewish silversmiths, embroiderers and bookmakers were central to Moroccan jewellery and textile production until emigration. | Jewish silversmiths, embroiderers and bookmakers were central to Moroccan jewellery and textile production until emigration, and a community of about 1,000 remains in Morocco. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Jews_in_Morocco\nCOM065 | making_significance | Kamba carvers developed a large export carving industry from the early 20th c. | Kamba carvers have run a large export carving industry since the early 20th c. and remain known for woodcarving. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamba_people\nCOM214 | making_significance | Bukharan Jewish dyers and embroiderers were central to ikat and suzani trades in Bukhara. | Bukharan Jewish dyers and embroiderers were central to ikat and suzani trades in Bukhara, and most of the community now lives in Israel and the United States. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bukharan_Jews\n",
"COM248 | making_significance | Sephardic communities made illuminated Hebrew manuscripts in Iberia and later ritual silver and textiles across the Ottoman world. | Sephardic communities made illuminated Hebrew manuscripts in Iberia and later ritual silver and textiles across the Ottoman world, and Sephardic communities live today chiefly in Israel, France and the Americas. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sephardic_Jews\nCOM284 | making_significance | Yapese voyagers quarried rai stone disks on Palau and brought them home as valuables. | Yapese voyagers quarried rai stone disks on Palau and brought them home as valuables, and rai are still exchanged in marriages, inheritance and other important transactions. | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rai_stones\n"
